WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.110 align:start position:0%
 
president steinmeier  secretary general guterres dr tedros  distinguished guests i'm honored  to be a patron of this year's world  health summit  and i'm delighted to be able to speak to  you today  even if i wish it could be in person  last year's edition with more than 2  500 participants and 300 speakers from  100 nations  feels very difficult to imagine only 12  months  on this event has long reflected the  global and interconnected nature of  health  in the 21st century and looking back  now this is something i perhaps  had not fully grasped as a student when  i saw medicine primarily through a  scientific lens  and while i started to understand it  more  as a doctor in obstetrics and gynecology  the nature of my work was still very  narrowly  focused on the clinical and  the individual it is important  but it was only later when working  at the department of epidemiology at  the hanover medical school that i really  learned what global health meant  and there's one story from the time  the story of an epidemiologist  that i think illustrates this better  than i could explain  it was back in february and march 2003  just as i was moving from medicine to  become state minister  with responsibility for health amongst  other things  at the exact same time on the other side  of the world  a sars super spreading event was taking  place  on the ninth floor of the metropole  hotel in hong kong  and within days the virus has  had spread as far as singapore canada  and vietnam  and it was in hanoi that dr  carlo urbani an infectious disease  specialist  was working for the who  here was an italian doctor  working in a french hospital in vietnam  treating an american patient who himself  had fallen sick in hong kong  he recognized at first that this  was a sars-like respiratory disease and  he was the first  to trigger the alarm and in doing so  he saved countless lives everywhere from  thailand to toronto  and probably far beyond  i mentioned this story firstly to pay  tribute to  dr urbani who as many of you will know  became infected shortly afterwards and  sadly passed away  leaving a young family behind  also to illustrate how linked our health  is  and how important global cooperation is  more than at any point in human history  the health of one country  directly affects that of another  and cross-border viruses need  cross-border responses  especially as we move into the next  phase of this pandemic  the dramatic spikes in recent weeks  across the world  and especially here in europe reflect  the picture  that is getting worse by the day so we  are back  to march and april levels and in many  cases already far beyond it  but while the virus is back stronger  than ever  our knowledge and our experience of what  we need to do  is also much better than before  and i would like to focus on three areas  where this will be critical in the next  month ahead of us  the first is the need for coordination  across  borders at the beginning  of the pandemic unilateral border  closures threatened  our ability to manage the health crisis  and our ability to keep our societies  and economies going  in response we created green lanes  to ensure the availability of goods and  essential services  but this pointed to the crucial  importance of early  and effective cross-border cooperation  the virus does not respect any borders  and this is why the commission last  month  set out how measures taken by member  states  should be coordinated and communicated  we now have common criteria and  thresholds when deciding whether to  introduce travel restrictions  we have a map with a single set of  colors  where everyone agrees which regions are  red  orange or green and we  now need a single set of rules for what  testing and quarantine people have to do  if they are traveling from a high-risk  zone  the second area where we need to draw on  our experience is the supply of  equipment  in response to shortages at the  beginning of the pandemic  europe took swift action to ensure that  industry here in europe  could ramp up the production of masks of  gloves  tests ventilators and all the other  essential equipment  we created a common european reserve for  medical equipment  and through our civil protection  mechanism that's how we call it  we can now ensure that it is distributed  to where it is needed most  like for example the 30 ventilators that  we are now sending  to czechia  also the need to diversify some of our  supply chains and critical areas  will be a key focus in the weeks and  months and years ahead of us  in the meantime we know that almost  every country  will take drastic and difficult measures  to contain the spread of the virus  rightly so people are once again being  asked  to make painful sacrifices we owe it to  them  to all pull in the same direction  to protect their lives and their  livelihoods  and this is the third area i would like  to discuss  we need to ensure that we support people  and that we support the economy  and this is why we created shure  this is a temporary instrument to keep  people  in work and in the companies in business  it will be funded by 100 billion euros  issued through social bonds and it will  now start supporting short time work  schemes  in at least 16 member states  and in parallel our recovery plan we  call it next generation eu  will finance 750 billion euros of  investment and reform  to kick-start our economy to strengthen  our health  systems and to accelerate europe's twin  green and digital transition  ladies and gentlemen  the point is we cannot wait for the end  of the pandemic  to repair and prepare for the future and  this is why  we will be putting forward proposals to  improve our ability to collectively  respond to serious cross-border health  threats we will strengthen our medicines  and scientific agencies  the ecdc and emma  and we will build a new european agency  for  biomedical advanced research and  development  and we will build the foundation of a  stronger european health union  in which 27 countries work together  to detect prepare and respond  collectively  on the one hand we have seen the limits  of what europe can do without competence  in this area  and we do need to have a real debate on  this  because on the other hand we have seen  the real added  value when countries cooperate and  coordinate on  everything from data to criteria  only this week the commission has set up  an eu-wide gateway  for national tracing apps to ensure they  safely  interoperate and interact with each  other  so tracing apps from germany ireland and  italy for example are now linked  to this gateway and czechia denmark  latvia spain and many others will soon  also follow this will help  people who work or travel across borders  and ensure we are all pulling together  all pulling together this must be the  hallmark of the european health union  and i believe this can be a test case  for  true global health compact  the need for leadership is clear and i  believe  the european union must assume this  responsibility  and this is why i will convene a global  health  summit next year alongside prime  minister conte  and the italian g20 presidency the aim  will be  to bring all global efforts together to  learn the lessons of the crisis  and to update global health cooperation  for this age of pandemics we  really need to rally around a set of  international commitments  investments and principles for the next  decade of global health cooperation  and we have been laying the groundwork  for this ever since the pandemic started  in april the european commission helped  launch the  act accelerator initiative  an initiative to globally coordinate and  speed up the development  and fair allocation of vaccines  treatments and diagnostics  then in may we brought more than 40  countries together  to raise the necessary 16 billion euros  again for universal access to tests  treatments and vaccines  and to support the economic recovery of  the world's most fragile regions and  communities  and this was a truly global effort  it was supported by many many for  example global citizen  the bill and melinda gates foundation  the welcome trust  the who the global preparedness  monitoring board just to name some  and then in august the european  commission joined the kovacs facility  it is co-hosted by gavi contributed  400 million euros for pooled  procurement and equitable distribution  of covered 19 vaccines  around the world but we all know there  is  way more work to do and we know that  developing a vaccine won't save lives in  itself  but vaccinations will  so we need to ensure that those who need  them most  urgently can get it first  the health workers the vulnerable the  older generation  the european commission has been working  hard to make  agreements with pharmaceutical companies  and secure future doses we must now get  ready  to deploy it both in europe with our  recent vaccination strategy  and of course around the world  and we will not stop once the first  vaccinations have been administered  because way more important is that we  will centrally collect  robust data and robust evidence  to assess the impact the effectiveness  and any side effects  of these vaccines national immunization  technical advisory groups  will in close cooperation with ecdc  and the ema play a key role  in the coordination of large-scale  eu-wide studies  that are monitoring the safety and the  effectiveness  of covet-19 vaccines  preparation for these activities must  start now they must start now to be in  place  when covet 19 vaccines reach the  european market  ladies and gentlemen only with trust  and with transparency about the process  will we overcome any doubts or  disinformation  but for the longer term i believe it is  time for the world  to change its mindset on health  we need to move away from looking after  our health  only through clinical individual  outcomes  in today's world we need to look after  our health  by looking after our planet our  well-being  and our sustainable development  and we need to do it together through  global health  cooperation and not through global  health competition  and this is the aim of this conference  and this is what you can count on europe  for  in the difficult period ahead and in the  decade  that follows thank you so much

